.TH VPNMAN 1 "2026" "vpnman 1.0.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vpnman \- multi-protocol VPN manager with a kill switch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vpnman
[\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]
.br
.B vpnmand
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run without arguments in a terminal for an interactive menu. \fBvpnmand\fR is the privileged background daemon
(managed by systemd, runit, OpenRC, SysV init or OpenBSD rc.d) that owns tunnels and the network lock;
\fBvpnman\fR and the GTK application talk to it over a Unix socket available to members of the \fBvpnman\fR group.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
status, list, connect, disconnect, import, add, remove, edit, fav, block, ping
Manage profiles and the connection.
.TP
lock on|off|status
Engage or release the network lock (kill switch).
.TP
schedule [list|add|remove|enable|disable|on|off]
Connect (and optionally disconnect) at set times; see vpnman schedule --help.
.TP
bypass [list|add|remove|available|on|off]
Applications that keep using the normal connection while the VPN is up (split tunnel, Linux).
.TP
routes [list|add|remove]
Addresses, networks or domains that always skip the VPN and are never blocked by the kill switch.
.TP
networks [show|trust|untrust]
The current network and trusted networks (automatic connect on untrusted ones).
.TP
leaktest
Check the live connection: tunnel, public IP, kill switch, DNS and IPv6 leaks.
.TP
history
Recent connections with duration and traffic.
.TP
backup export|import FILE
Back up or restore all profiles (including credentials) and settings.
.TP
cleanup
Remove firewall rules, routes and cgroups VPNMan left behind (root).
.TP
get, set
Read or change settings (dotted keys such as netlock.allow_lan).
.TP
logs [-f], protocols, doctor, service, daemon, gui
Diagnostics, service control and the GTK interface.
.SH FILES
.TP
/etc/vpnman/
Profiles, settings and state (root only).
.TP
/run/vpnman/vpnman.sock
Daemon socket.
